# How much of its period every supply is actually available for, or the typed reason it files no
# such figure. One row per nameplate.
#
# A second question beside the whole unit
#   A supply can arrive in whole units and be available only part of the time, both at once: a
#   truck that comes twice a week is both. So how much comes and when it comes are separate columns,
#   and the window is never a third answer to the first question.
#
# Three things a blank could mean
#   A blank would mean three things at once, which is why it is typed, and only two of the three
#   are absences.
#     one whole period   the supply runs all the time. It is filed as the value it is, `1 week`
#                        against a period of `week`, with an origin saying who could shorten it.
#                        Filed as `absent` with the reason `none`, it would lose its size and its
#                        origin.
#     `notApplicable`    the unit has no period, so "five days of what?" has no answer.
#     `unmeasured`       nobody asked.
#   Only the last one stops a calculation: a time slack cannot be worked out from a clearance when
#   nobody knows whether the spare is spread evenly over the period.
#
# No stated amount required
#   The window says when the supply is there, and a supply nobody has sized still has one, so this
#   relation reads nameplates whether or not they state an amount.
-- pm:Nameplate/pm:Divisibility/pm:window, beside the amount unit that decides if it is answerable.
SELECT n.filing, n.layer,
       n.window_low, n.window_mode, n.window_high, n.window_unit, n.window_size_absent,
       n.window_absent,
       n.amount_unit
FROM pm.nameplate n
